              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.16: Measuring cylinder of 100 mL"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.083,
            0.882,
            0.12
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z How many smaller divisions are there between the two bigger marks?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.12,
            0.811,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z How much volume does one small division indicate?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.203,
            0.415,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why are measuring cylinders always designed narrow and tall instead of wider and short like a beaker?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.144,
            0.882,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The smallest volume that the measuring cylinder can read is_"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.428,
            0.182,
            0.883,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For the measuring cylinder shown in Fig. 9.16, the volume difference indicated between 10 mL and 20 mL, or between 40 mL and 50 mL, is 10 mL."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.43,
            0.256,
            0.882,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The number of divisions between these marks is 10."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.063,
            0.344,
            0.239,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.063,
                0.344,
                0.239,
                0.869
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.294,
            0.739,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, one small division can read 10 ÷ 10 = 1 mL."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.315,
            0.882,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "That is, the smallest value that this measuring cylinder can read is 1 mL."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.353,
            0.884,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The smallest volume that a measuring cylinder can measure depends on the capacity of the measuring cylinder. Usually it is 0.1 mL in smaller measuring cylinders with a capacity of 10 mL or 25 mL, it is 1 mL in a 100 mL measuring cylinder, 2 mL in a 250 mL measuring cylinder, and 5 mL in a 500 mL measuring cylinder. Suppose we want to take 70 mL of water. If we use a 50 mL measuring cylinder, it would not be possible to measure 70 mL of water in one step. First, we have to measure 50 mL water and then 20 mL. Measuring volume in more than one step is not convenient. On the other hand, if a 250 mL or 500 mL measuring cylinder is used, the measurement can be done in one step but the accuracy would be reduced as the smallest volume that these measuring cylinders can measure is greater than that of a 100 mL measuring cylinder. Hence, a 100 mL measuring cylinder is the best choice for this measurement."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.644,
            0.838,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 9.5: Let us measure 50 mL of water"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.679,
            0.831,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Place a clean, dry measuring cylinder on a flat surface."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.701,
            0.882,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pour water slowly into the measuring cylinder up to the required mark, as shown in Fig. 9.17."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.738,
            0.883,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z If required, adjust the level of water in the measuring cylinder by adding or removing a small amount of water using a dropper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.795,
            0.883,
            0.849
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "On careful observation, you will notice that the water inside the measuring cylinder forms a curved surface. This curved surface is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "meniscus",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 9.18)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.273,
            0.851,
            0.883,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Read the mark on the measuring cylinder that coincides with the bottom of the meniscus for water or other colourless liquids."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.001,
            0.923,
